\title{Which TPU axes actually wrap around}
\taxon{Card}
\tag{card}
\meta{anki}{1786395975892}
\meta{anki-deck}{MLSys}

\prompt{[[O14H]] topology: which generations get which [torus](DDKT), and when do you actually get wraparound links?}{
  \ul{
    \li{\strong{2D torus}, 4 neighbors — v2, v3, v5e, v6e. A pod caps at #{16 \times 16}; past that, pods talk over DCN.}
    \li{\strong{3D torus}, 6 neighbors — v4, v5p. Superpods are #{16 \times 16 \times 16} (v4) and #{16 \times 20 \times 28} (v5p), built from reconfigurable #{4 \times 4 \times 4} cubes joined by optical links.}
  }

  \strong{Wraparounds} exist only on full-cube multiples (#{4 \times 4 \times 4}, #{4 \times 4 \times 8} — \em{not} #{2 \times 2 \times 4}, since the optical switches only exist on a full cube), or on a v5e/v6e axis of exactly 16, so an #{8 \times 16} slice wraps the long axis only. Losing the wraparound roughly \strong{doubles} comms time.

  Links are nearest-neighbor only and bypass the hosts entirely, so distant chips hop through the chips between them — unlike a GPU's switch hierarchy.
}
